{
  "id": "15-dignity-and-connection",
  "title": "Dignity and Connection",
  "tenet": "Meet loneliness with love and truth",
  "durationMs": 80400,
  "audioAsset": "kingdom/15-dignity-and-connection.mp3",
  "transcriptAsset": "assets/kingdom/15-dignity-and-connection.json",
  "text": "When loneliness sends you toward a screen, whose company do you actually need?\n\nA young man worked late, ate alone, and reached for his phone when the house fell quiet. He had set a filter, but every hard evening still felt the same. One night he paused over an image and remembered that the person before him had a name, relationships, hopes, and a life beyond his gaze. He closed the page. Then he sent a simple message to a trusted friend: Could we talk tomorrow? The reply came before he went to sleep. The next day they walked together. The filter still mattered, but friendship answered a need it could not touch.\n\nPornography can turn a person into an object in our minds. Whoever appears on a screen retains their dignity; we do not know their story, and another person's clothing or choices do not carry our responsibility. Paul calls us to honor God with our bodies. When isolation makes temptation stronger, guard the hour and seek real connection. Put the phone away, leave the room, call a trusted person, or join others in honest service. Name loneliness without shame. If this pattern keeps taking control, seek compassionate, skilled support. What is one relationship you can strengthen today? Choose one practical boundary and one human connection. Let both serve love.",
